      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. Remember the Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this case, we are given two sides of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm. Let's call the remaining side c. According to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n6^2 + 8^2 = c^2\n36 + 64 = c^2\n100 = c^2\n\nNow, we can take the square root of both sides to solve for c:\n\nc = √100\nc = 10\n\nSo, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is 10 cm.",
