      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "+1 Metal A\n6 cm\n8 cm\nDraw an altitude from the right angle to the hypotenuse, and call the foot of the altitude $P$. By the Pythagorean Theorem, $9 = \\left(2P\\right)^2 + \\left(3P\\right)^2$,\nor $9 = 4P^2 + 9P^2$. Solving for $P^2$, we get $9 = 13P^2$, so $P^2 = \\frac{9}{13}$. Therefore, $P = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}$. Recall that the shortest side of the triangle is an altitude. Therefore, the length of the shortest side of the triangle is $\\boxed{2.29}$ centimeters. The answer is: 2.29",
